WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.430
This is a safety log for construction crews.

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:08.370
It works offline, it's driven by voice, and nothing in it can be quietly changed.

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:11.880
A field note starts at the mic.

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:14.940
This build transcribes it on the laptop's own graphics card,

00:00:14.990 --> 00:00:18.060
with no network, so the note never leaves the device.

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:20.120
Here's one from a highway job.

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:21.340
I'll start recording.

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:26.250
Crew was on the shoulder next to live traffic, setting up a lane closure.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:27.950
Flagger was in position.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.990
We had an open trench along the median, with no shoring in yet.

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:35.660
When I stop, it comes back as text in about three seconds,

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:37.300
without leaving this laptop.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:40.170
Every word came through.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:43.810
The raw transcript is kept untouched, beside the cleaned-up version,

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:47.730
and any summary is shown alongside it, never in place of it.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:51.750
I set the trade to highway and bridge, and confirm the details.

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:57.860
Two flags: work-zone traffic control, and an open trench.

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:01.320
For highway work, forty-one percent of inspections are cited,

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:04.780
at about twenty-one thousand seven hundred dollars each.

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:07.180
I accept both, and save.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:11.290
The record is saved, and its audit verifies.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:16.010
Resolving the trench flag takes a note, appended to the chain as a new version.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:17.890
And the note can be spoken, too.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.750
Shoring box dropped in at two p.m.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:23.770
The competent person signed off.

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:29.870
A couple of seconds later, it's in the note field, and I resolve with proof.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:31.610
The fix lands as version two,

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:35.210
above the original record, which stays exactly as it was.

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:36.790
Nothing is overwritten.

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:42.100
Every change is chained and hash-verified: the record,

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:46.340
its audio, and the fix, three entries under one head hash.

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:49.260
The same records feed a trade-risk dashboard,

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:53.320
benchmarked against two years of OSHA enforcement in the New York area,

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:55.660
with open flags counted by site.

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:59.720
Each entry's hash includes the one before it,

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:02.660
so nothing can be silently altered or removed.

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:07.260
HouseCheck's export uses this same chain, two cycles later.

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:09.000
It was proven here first.
